            <ArticleTitle>The effect of low-glycemic carbohydrate on insulin and glucose response in vivo and in vitro in patients with coronary heart disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>669-72</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The insulin resistance syndrome has recently been implicated in the etiology of coronary heart disease, with a possible metabolic defect at the level of the adipocyte. We report the effects of a low- versus high-glycemic-index (LGI and HGI, respectively) diet on insulin and glucose response as assessed by oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) and insulin-stimulated glucose uptake in isolated adipocytes in a group of 32 patients with advanced coronary heart disease. The area under the insulin curve following OGTT was significantly reduced after 4 weeks in the LGI group (P &lt; .03), but not in the HGI group. Insulin-stimulated glucose uptake in isolated adipocytes harvested from a presternal fat biopsy was significantly greater following the LGI diet (P &lt; .05). This study demonstrates that simple short-term dietary measures can improve insulin sensitivity in patients with coronary heart disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
